  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1245 PM MST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nTuesday and Wednesday...Models have been fairly consistent on the \nnext fast-moving system to affect the region. An upper level open \nwave will cross the Great Basin Tuesday, bringing a quick hit of \nsnow to the mts Tuesday evening through Wednesday morning before \nejecting up across WY and MT. Most of the mt peaks may see up to 2 \ninches of new snow through the event, but the Continental Divide \nwill see the greatest amounts, and the eastern San Juan Mts may see \nas much as 5 to 8 inches which are advisory amounts. If that trend \nremains consistent, then forecasters either tonight or tomorrow may \nneed to issue a highlight. Across the plains, conditions remain dry \nthough the increased W-SW downslope flow should help boost temps \nboth days up to 5 to 10 degrees above seasonal normals. For Tue, \nexpect highs in the 50s for the high valleys and 60s across the \nplains. On Wed, Temps cool for the high valleys into the 40s to \naround 50F, with mid 50s to mid 60s for the plains.\n\nThursday and Friday...Another fast-moving upper level feature moves \nin Thursday night through Friday, bringing another quick shot of \nsnow to the higher terrain as well as a bit more winds for the \nentire forecast area. The models the past few days had been in stark \ncontrast with one another on how to deal with this next system, but \nare now starting to come into alignment, agreeing on a more \nnortherly and quick track. Initial snow amounts and locations are \nshaping up be a repeat of what has occurred recently and what is \nexpected for Tue night. As for temps,  dry cold front dropping south \nacross the eastern plains late Wednesday night will drop temps a few \ndegrees across the plains on Thursday, keeping the plains at or \nstill slightly above seasonal norms. Friday will be the warmest day \nof the extended forecast as the increased western flow pushes highs \nacross the plains into the 60s to near 70F.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...Once again temporary ridging aloft will mean a \nshort period of dry conditions and lighter winds, with seasonal \ntemps forecast. Moore",
